Experiment 2: Buffer Overflow Vulnerability and Mitigation
Subject
Foundation of Cyber Security

Aim
To understand buffer overflow vulnerability in C, demonstrate its effect in a controlled environment, and study mitigation strategies.

Vulnerable C Program
#include <stdio.h>
#include <string.h>

int main() {
    char buffer[10];

    printf("Enter a string: ");
    gets(buffer);

    printf("You entered: %s\n", buffer);

    return 0;
}
Explanation
The program contains a character array named buffer with a size of 10 bytes.

The gets() function does not check the size of the input. If the user enters more characters than the buffer can hold, the extra data can overwrite adjacent memory.

This condition is known as a Buffer Overflow.

Vulnerable Function
gets(buffer);
The gets() function is unsafe because it does not perform input length checking.

Demonstration
When input exceeds the allocated buffer size, the program may produce unexpected behavior or crash.

This demonstrates the basic effect of a buffer overflow vulnerability.

Mitigation
A safer version of the program uses fgets():

#include <stdio.h>

int main() {
    char buffer[10];

    printf("Enter a string: ");
    fgets(buffer, sizeof(buffer), stdin);

    printf("You entered: %s", buffer);

    return 0;
}
Mitigation Strategies
Avoid unsafe functions such as gets().
Use bounded input functions such as fgets().
Validate input length before processing it.
Use compiler security protections such as stack canaries.
Enable modern memory protection mechanisms such as ASLR and NX/DEP.
Perform regular code review and security testing.
Result
The buffer overflow vulnerability was studied using a vulnerable C program, and appropriate mitigation techniques were implemented.

Conclusion
Buffer overflow occurs when more data is written to a buffer than it can hold. Using safe input functions, proper input validation, and security mechanisms helps prevent buffer overflow vulnerabilities.

